  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 127 PM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nContinued uncertainty expected in the long term forecast due to\nmodel variance with differing amounts of precipitation \npossible, but no matter what a system will move through the \nregion and bring strong winds with temperatures likely dropping \nas this moves through the region. Thursday into Friday the upper\nlevel transient ridge will continue it's progression through \nthe region as it makes way for a strong Pacific trough and jet \nthat will start to overtake by the end of the week. At the \nsurface we'll see another dry and warm day for the Thanksgiving \nholiday with incoming cloudiness in response to the approaching \nsystem. On Friday upslope flow across the mountains increases, \nallowing wintry precipitation to resume for them. Winds \nmeanwhile will ramp up late Thursday into Friday, and continue \nthrough Saturday before finally lessening, likely bringing a \nmulti-day high wind event for our wind prone locations. Most \nguidance is in solid agreement on this already, with in-house \nmodels showing a 40-60% probability of high winds for the wind \nprones, and 850 mb pressure gradients of 60 to 80 meters, giving\nhigh confidence to the likelihood of this event.\n\nWhat's less likely is precipitation outside of the mountains,\nwith a wide swath of outcomes from all ensemble members.\nEverything from our usual dry conditions to Cheyenne seeing near\n6 inches of snowfall is on the table. The higher precipitation\nchance scenarios also bring with them cooler temperatures, while\nthe drier scenarios from the system just a bit further east\nbring warmer temperatures. As of this writing I would say\nensembles seem to be favoring the lower elevations finally\ngetting at least some meager precipitation, so hopefully\nCheyenne will finally see it's first noteworthy accumulating\nsnowfall this record setting year, but to what amount is still\nup in the air.\n\nMoving into Sunday and the start of next week we should briefly\ndry out with cooler temperatures on tap, warming back into\nMonday as another shortwave passes across the region. This one\nwill yet again feature the strongest forcing well to our south,\nso expect any precipitation to be limited once again to the\nmountains from orographic enhancement. Overall an active end to\nNovember, and hopefully we can finally achieve some\nprecipitation for our incredibly dry eastern zones.",
